1. Reference method and code (Friedman et al. / ml4h)
-----------------------------------------------------
The unsupervised median-beat autoencoder reproduced here follows:

  Friedman, S.F., et al. "Unsupervised deep learning of electrocardiograms
  enables scalable human disease profiling." npj Digital Medicine 8, 23 (2025).

The reference implementation is the Broad Institute "ml4h" toolkit
(https://github.com/broadinstitute/ml4h), licensed under the GNU General Public
License v3.0 (GPL-3.0).

models/autoencoder.py reproduces the published median-beat autoencoder
ARCHITECTURE faithfully (parameter-exact: same layers, kernels and parameter
count), but its CODE is written independently in PyTorch. The distinction is
deliberate:

  * The architecture (network design and parameter count) is Friedman et al. /
    ml4h's, and is reproduced on purpose. A network architecture is a functional
    design and is not itself subject to copyright.
  * The code that implements it is original expression, written from the published
    description and by inspecting the serialized reference model files (layer
    shapes and parameter counts). It does NOT incorporate or translate ml4h
    source code.

Reproducing an (uncopyrightable) design parameter-for-parameter does not make the
implementing code a derivative work of ml4h's GPL-3.0 source. ml4h is credited as
the source of the architecture and reference weights. No ml4h source code and no
ml4h-trained reference weights are included or redistributed. This repository's own
code is licensed under the GNU GPL, version 3 or later — the copyleft license of ml4h — so
any redistribution or derivative work must also remain under the GPL (see LICENSE).


2. Data sources (not included)
------------------------------
No datasets, no patient data, and no data-derived intermediate files (caches,
decoded waveforms, extracted features, trained weights) are included in this
repository. It does include aggregated result tables (AUCs, confidence intervals,
P values and counts per finding, in results/paper/ and experiments/stats/) and, for
the openly licensed PhysioNet/CinC 2017 data only, the reference labels and per-record
out-of-fold probabilities without record names (results/paper/handheld_oof.npz), and
the signals of one openly licensed PTB-XL recording (CC BY 4.0), without its record
name, for Fig. 1 (results/paper/fig1_example_ptbxl.npz). Users must obtain the data themselves under the
applicable terms. All are distributed via PhysioNet (Goldberger et al.,
Circulation 101(23):e215, 2000):

  * MIMIC-IV-ECG — credentialed access under the PhysioNet Credentialed Health
    Data License 1.5.0 and Data Use Agreement; redistribution prohibited. Used for
    self-supervised encoder pre-training and for the recordings of the
    hospital-diagnosis cohort.
      Gow, B., et al. MIMIC-IV-ECG (2023).
        https://physionet.org/content/mimic-iv-ecg/

  * MIMIC-IV, hosp module (version 3.1) — credentialed access under the same
    license and Data Use Agreement; redistribution prohibited. Used for age, sex,
    race, admissions and the ICD-coded hospital diagnoses.
      Johnson, A.E.W., et al. MIMIC-IV (2024).
        https://physionet.org/content/mimiciv/

  * PhysioNet/CinC 2017 Challenge — open access. Handheld single-lead atrial
    fibrillation.
      Clifford, G.D., et al. (2017).
        https://physionet.org/content/challenge-2017/

  * Chapman-Shaoxing + Ningbo (`ecg-arrhythmia`) — open access. The first
    external cohort: 13 physician-annotated findings, one to twelve leads.
      Zheng, J., et al. Scientific Data 7, 48 (2020).
        https://physionet.org/content/ecg-arrhythmia/

  * PTB-XL (version 1.0.3) — open access. The second external cohort.
      Wagner, P., et al. Scientific Data 7, 154 (2020).
        https://physionet.org/content/ptb-xl/

  * PhysioNet/CinC Challenge 2021 (version 1.0.3) — open access. The SNOMED-CT
    codes of the PTB-XL statements.
      Reyna, M.A., et al. Computing in Cardiology 48 (2021).
        https://physionet.org/content/challenge-2021/

  * Icentia11k — open access. Adhesive-patch single-lead pre-training corpus.
      Tan, S., et al. (2019).
        https://physionet.org/content/icentia11k-continuous-ecg/

  * MIT-BIH Noise Stress Test Database (`nstdb`) — open access. Real recorded
    noise for the robustness analysis.
      Moody, G.B., et al. Computers in Cardiology 11, 381 (1984).
        https://physionet.org/content/nstdb/

No restricted data or data derivatives are redistributed in this repository.


3. Model weights are intentionally NOT shared
---------------------------------------------
Trained model weights are not included. The encoders were pre-trained on credentialed
MIMIC-IV-ECG data under the PhysioNet Data Use Agreement, and the learned
representations encode patient-stable structure; releasing the weights openly could
expose information derived from credentialed data. They are available on request to
researchers with credentialed MIMIC-IV access, and credentialed users can pre-train
the models again from the code in this repository.


4. Phecode map
--------------
The ICD-coded hospital diagnoses are defined through the public Phecode map v1.2
(Wu, P., et al. JMIR Medical Informatics 7(4):e14325, 2019; https://phewascatalog.org).
The map itself is not included.
